You are a senior staff-level full-stack engineer, platform architect, security engineer, UX designer, DevOps engineer, and technical writer. Build a production-quality, self-hosted web application named NachtLabs.

NachtLabs is a web-based AI dark-factory control plane. It accepts software work requests, orchestrates AI coding agents, connects to source-control repositories, performs a governed workflow from plan through pull request, and provides deep monitoring, logs, alerts, auditability, and human-reviewed reliability recommendations.

The application must feel like a premium developer tool and operations console. It must not be a generic CRUD admin dashboard, a prototype, a static mockup, or a collection of unconnected UI screens. Build functional vertical slices with real authentication, PostgreSQL persistence, permissions, API contracts, integrations, audit records, durable job state, logs, monitoring, and tests.

NachtLabs is Linux-first. Its canonical development, testing, and production environment is Linux. It runs directly as native services managed by systemd. Version one must not require or include Docker, Docker Compose, Kubernetes, container images, container manifests, or container-runtime assumptions. Docker support may be evaluated later only after the core factory is stable.

Do not create Git commits or push the NachtLabs application repository unless the operator explicitly asks you to do so. The operator will manually manage source control for NachtLabs itself. This does not apply to the product functionality: authorized NachtLabs runs must still be able to create branches, commits, pushes, and pull requests in connected target GitHub and Gitea repositories.

============================================================
1. PRODUCT DEFINITION
============================================================

Product name: NachtLabs

Positioning:
NachtLabs is a premium developer tool for safely orchestrating AI software-engineering work. It provides a central operations console and a robust API through which humans and authorized agents can submit work, observe progress, inspect evidence, configure integrations, and govern automated changes.

Primary integration targets for the first release:
- Hermes Agent
- OpenCode
- Direct Ollama server connections
- GitHub
- Gitea

Create a provider-neutral architecture so other LLM providers, coding agents, Git hosts, CI systems, deployment systems, execution runners, notification services, and observability backends can be added later without redesigning the application.

Initial operating posture:
- Start with one organization, one project, one worker, and bounded concurrency of one active implementation run.
- Allow the data model and architecture to support multiple organizations, projects, workers, and concurrent runs later.
- Do not enable unattended schedules, automatic merges, automatic deployments, destructive actions, or elevated privilege workflows by default.
- New projects begin in plan-approval-required, PR-only mode.
- The first end-to-end goal is one small, bounded, safe change with recorded evidence—not broad autonomous development.

Core user journey:
1. An operator installs and runs NachtLabs on Linux as native systemd-managed services.
2. The first created account automatically becomes the Owner.
3. The Owner signs in and configures the organization and security policy.
4. The Owner connects a local or remote Ollama server.
5. The Owner configures Hermes Agent and/or OpenCode.
6. The Owner connects a GitHub or Gitea repository.
7. The Owner defines the project Mission and at least one Validation Journey.
8. The Owner creates a structured work request in the web UI.
9. NachtLabs validates the request, evaluates it against the Mission, and discovers the repository.
10. NachtLabs produces an implementation plan, risk summary, affected-file estimate, test plan, acceptance-criteria mapping, Mission-alignment assessment, and Validation Journey impact assessment.
11. The Owner or authorized Operator approves the plan.
12. NachtLabs creates an isolated workspace and feature branch, delegates implementation to an enabled agent, runs configured validation, performs bounded repair attempts, runs independent verification, pushes the branch, and creates a GitHub or Gitea pull request.
13. The user watches the run in real time and can inspect structured logs, tool calls, Git commits, tests, artifacts, model metadata, verification findings, approvals, and audit events.
14. NachtLabs Monitoring & Insights detects recurring non-sensitive issues, explains evidence, recommends safe remediation, and requires human approval before applying any configuration-changing action.

