3. NON-NEGOTIABLE ENGINEERING RULES
============================================================

Build a real, maintainable application.

Do:
- Implement real API endpoints, database models, migrations, authorization checks, frontend data fetching, validation, background jobs, integration adapters, and error handling.
- Build incrementally in coherent milestones and keep the application runnable after every milestone.
- Use durable, explicit PostgreSQL state for workflows, run status, approvals, audit events, API keys, integrations, secret metadata, configuration, monitoring events, Missions, Validation Journeys, holdouts, verification findings, and remediation history.
- Use secure defaults.
- Write architecture and operations documentation as the project evolves.
- Include automated tests for essential business logic, permissions, API behavior, workflow transitions, secret masking, Git behavior, Mission enforcement, Validation Journey execution, holdout access control, verifier decisions, regression policy, and critical operational flows.
- Use semantic design tokens and accessible UI patterns.
- Make all code strongly typed where the language supports it.
- Provide clean commands for installation, development, testing, linting, type checking, formatting, migrations, seed data, backup, restore, production startup, service installation, and health checks.
- Use stable interfaces and dependency injection around external providers.
- Store secrets encrypted at rest and never expose plaintext secrets after creation.
- Use structured logs, telemetry correlation, and stable request/run/trace identifiers.
- Preserve audit data independently from operational logs.
- Update relevant documentation whenever behavior, architecture, configuration, operations, or security controls change.

Do not:
- Do not fake integrations with hard-coded “success” data in production code.
- Do not place API keys, OAuth tokens, passwords, private keys, session secrets, credentials, or plaintext environment files in source control, browser local storage, logs, client-side bundles, screenshots, error responses, test fixtures, or generated artifacts.
- Do not let the browser call Ollama, GitHub, Gitea, Hermes Agent, OpenCode, or sensitive third-party integrations directly.
- Do not build monolithic unmaintainable components, routes, services, workers, or database modules.
- Do not scatter literal color values throughout components. Use semantic design tokens.
- Do not use unsafe shell execution without command allowlisting, isolated working directories, strict timeouts, bounded output, safe argument handling, process controls, and audit logging.
- Do not let agents merge pull requests, deploy, alter authorization, change secrets, access unapproved network destinations, run destructive commands, or change protected policies without explicit policy and required human approval.
- Do not automatically “self-improve” settings, prompts, credentials, workflows, source code, infrastructure, or production behavior from logs without a human approval record.
- Do not claim a workflow stage was completed unless evidence was actually recorded.
- Do not weaken security controls merely to make an integration easier.
- Do not create Git commits or push the NachtLabs application repository unless the operator explicitly directs you to do so.

When requirements are unclear:
- Choose the safest well-reasoned default.
- Record the decision in documentation or an Architecture Decision Record.
- Prefer a production-safe implementation over a flashy but fragile one.

