15. SAFETY, SANDBOXING, AND EXECUTION CONTROLS
============================================================

NachtLabs is an agent-control platform. Treat execution safety as a core product concern.

Baseline permissions:
- Agents may inspect configured repository content, analyze code, create plans, work in isolated run workspaces, and run explicitly configured safe validation commands.
- Agents may create branches, commit changes, push authorized branches, and create GitHub/Gitea pull requests when project policy permits.
- Merging, production deployment, destructive commands, database migrations, secrets access, external network actions, identity/access-control changes, and policy changes require human approval by default.

Implement:
- Per-project command allowlists.
- Configurable command timeouts.
- Process-tree termination on timeout/cancel.
- Bounded captured output with a full-output artifact strategy when safely required.
- Isolated per-run Linux workspace directories.
- Dedicated execution-service account with restricted permissions.
- Safe directory-cleanup policy.
- Validated workspace paths.
- Configurable network-policy abstraction.
- Future-ready sandbox/execution-runner interface.
- Linux-native local execution for version one, but do not portray it as a complete security sandbox.
- Prominent documentation describing the trust boundary and recommending OS-level isolation, dedicated hosts/VMs, low-privilege service accounts, restricted filesystem access, and appropriate network controls for production.
- Optional “manual intervention required” states for steps that cannot be safely automated.
- Never execute arbitrary shell content supplied directly from untrusted work-request text.
- Never interpolate untrusted user or agent content into shell commands without robust argument handling and allowlisting.

Make all sensitive or dangerous transitions explicit in the audit log.

