21. END-TO-END ACCEPTANCE MILESTONE
============================================================

The first fully demonstrated milestone must prove a real secure vertical slice.

It must work like this:

1. An operator installs and runs NachtLabs on Linux as native services.
2. No Docker, Docker Compose, Kubernetes, or container runtime is required.
3. PostgreSQL, API, worker, and web services are installed and managed through documented Linux/systemd procedures.
4. The developer creates the first account, which becomes Owner.
5. The Owner can sign in securely.
6. The Owner can select any of the six complete themes.
7. The Owner creates a project Mission with scope, non-goals, constraints, and a required validation baseline.
8. The Owner creates at least one Validation Journey for the project.
9. The Owner configures a local or remote Ollama endpoint and successfully tests connectivity.
10. The Owner configures at least one coding-agent adapter: Hermes Agent or OpenCode.
11. The Owner connects GitHub or self-hosted Gitea.
12. The Owner selects a repository and creates a NachtLabs project.
13. The Owner configures a safe validation command for the project.
14. The Owner creates one small bounded work request through the web UI.
15. NachtLabs performs intake validation, Mission/scope assessment, and repository discovery.
16. NachtLabs creates and displays a plan with affected files, risks, test plan, acceptance-criteria mapping, Mission alignment, and affected Validation Journeys.
17. The Owner approves the plan.
18. NachtLabs creates an isolated Linux workspace and feature branch.
19. NachtLabs invokes the configured agent adapter to make a small safe change, such as a documentation or tightly scoped code change.
20. NachtLabs runs configured checks, a secret scan, and at least one required Validation Journey.
21. NachtLabs invokes an independently configured verifier stage and displays its structured result.
22. NachtLabs records command evidence, logs, tool calls, model/agent data, artifacts, Git evidence, commit/push outcomes, Mission assessment, Validation Journey evidence, verifier findings, and state transitions.
23. NachtLabs creates a target-repository commit, pushes the feature branch, and opens a real GitHub or Gitea pull request.
24. NachtLabs displays PR link/status in run details.
25. NachtLabs records immutable audit events for all material actions.
26. Monitoring & Insights displays correlated run and system logs.
27. A deliberately induced non-sensitive test failure or unavailable test endpoint produces a visible incident/alert.
28. NachtLabs groups related errors and generates an evidence-backed remediation recommendation.
29. The recommendation requires human approval before any configuration-changing action.
30. The UI clearly distinguishes verified evidence from inferred root cause/recommendation.
31. A manually triggered regression run can execute a configured Validation Journey and display results.
32. NachtLabs services run under documented non-root systemd service accounts with defined filesystem restrictions and health checks.
33. The NachtLabs repository itself remains uncommitted/unpushed unless the operator explicitly asks for those actions.

Do not call this milestone complete until it works through actual UI, API, PostgreSQL database, worker, systemd service, and integration boundaries. If an external agent adapter cannot be fully exercised in the environment, implement a clearly labeled local development adapter that follows the exact production adapter contract and demonstrate the full orchestration pipeline against it. Do not hide this limitation; document it.

